A||||4||johnmac||Oil Pan Gasket & Permatex application||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||11-02-2000||01:47 AM||||I am planning to put back in my oil pan on my 2.8L. It was a bear to remove!<P>Question -<BR>Can I put permatax high temp. sealer between my pan and cork gasket? Let it set up overnight and then put it in? I am trying to avoid the potential misalignment of the gasket in placing the pan back in.<P>I don't plan to put permatex between the cork gasket and engine as it would be a mess later on to remove. Is this a correct procedure?<P>Help,<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-02-2000||06:04 PM||||I use RTV (silicone gasket sealer High temp) on both sides so it won't be a problem to remove if needed. Install WET and don't over tighten!||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||11-02-2000||09:27 PM||||I recommend tossing the cork gasket and getting the rubber upgrade gasket. In '87 GM went to a rubber pan gasket and they are now available for the older style pans as well. Mine cost $18 at Autozone for the '87. No RTV required with the rubber gasket, and they seal much better. The Fel-pro gasket also comes with some cool screw-in clips that hold the gasket in place on the engine while you put the pan up.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-02-2000||11:13 PM||||I use spray on permatex High Tack gasket adhesive for most things. <P>On a multipiece cork/silicon pan gasket you always put RTV in all the joints. I use the hightack spay to hold the pices in place. You could use RTV. I just find the spray much easier to use and it won't ever make boogers that could clog something up. (I like Permatex Super 300 and Balkamp Aviation sealler for allot fo the same reasons. Those are paint on.)<P>I've never worked with the rubber one. You can still use hightack on it to help keep it located if need be. I don't know if you need or want RTV on it. (I did use the rubber valve cover gasket on my 4 cyl. Very nice. Used High tack to keep it in place.)<P>Main thing is that everything must be clean and free of oil film. Use brake cleaner as the last cleaning step. No sealler works right if there's an oil film on the parts. (Most gasket removers and carb cleaners leave a film. Brake cleaner will remove it.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnmac||11-03-2000||05:14 AM||||Thanks fellows.<P>Anybody want a cork gasket. I am headed to Autozone for a rubber gasket. <P>Heard so many horror stories about leaking oil pan gaskets (mine was leaking before too) and I don't want to take the pan ever again in my lifetime!!!!<P>Thanks,<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||1
